---
intent: >
  Sweep every `/aide:init` reference out of `src/cli/init/` (orchestrator,
  renderer, MCP-entry writer, spec body, tests, types) and replace each
  occurrence with the per-category follow-up surface the updated spec
  ratifies — `/aide` for vault path (orchestrator inline-recovery),
  `npx aidemd-mcp init --ide <choice>` for IDE, `/aide:upgrade` for drift in
  shipped artifacts. Then sweep the same string out of the five sibling
  files outside this module that no other plan owns (README + four JSDoc
  blocks). After this plan executes, zero references to the deprecated
  slash command remain anywhere in the repo.
---

## Project Structure

This plan is reference-cleanup, not structural change. Every file already
exists; nothing is created, nothing is deleted, no helpers are extracted.
The scope tree is identical to the prior plan's post-state — every file is
modified in place to remove `/aide:init` strings and re-aim the user at the
right per-category follow-up surface.

```
src/cli/init/
├── .aide                              ← intent spec (already rewritten by spec-writer; body sweep
│                                         in step 6 — Context, Strategy, Good/Bad examples still
│                                         carry `/aide:init`)
├── plan.aide                          ← THIS plan (replaces the prior 23/23 plan)
├── todo.aide                          ← prior-cycle QA log (do not touch)
│
├── index.ts                           ← MODIFY (orchestrator):
│                                         runInit(cwd: string, write?: (line: string) => void, options?: { vaultPath?: string }): Promise<number>
│                                         Edits: deferredCategories() return strings (lines 36–42),
│                                         JSDoc lines 24, 27, the all-clean completion line at 225,
│                                         the --help text at 263–270. Top-level IIFE shape unchanged.
├── index.test.ts                      ← MODIFY: update the all-clean completion-line assertion at
│                                         line 293 to match the new wording chosen in step 1; verify
│                                         every other assertion still passes after the deferred-
│                                         categories array shape changes (the constant DEFERRED_CATEGORIES
│                                         at lines 90–94 needs the new strings the orchestrator will
│                                         pass to renderWarning).
│
├── types/                             ← (unchanged)
│   └── index.ts                       ← Shared types — no `/aide:init` references; do not touch.
│
├── writeMcpEntry/                     ← MODIFY (in-scope user-facing text):
│   ├── index.ts                       ← Edit the JSDoc at lines 19 and 25 and the runtime message
│   │                                    appended at line 105 ("obsidian vault path is a placeholder;
│   │                                    run /aide:init to set it"). New runtime message must name
│   │                                    the orchestrator's inline-recovery surface (`/aide`) per the
│   │                                    spec's per-category mapping. Signature unchanged:
│   │                                    writeMcpEntry(projectRoot: string, vaultPath?: string)
│   │                                      → Promise<{ status: "created" | "exists", message: string }>
│   └── index.test.ts                  ← MODIFY ONLY IF a test currently asserts the substring
│                                        "/aide:init" inside the returned message; otherwise leave
│                                        untouched. Step 2 verifies first, edits second.
│
└── renderWarning/                     ← MODIFY (heavy lift — wording is the user's primary signal):
    ├── index.ts                       ← Edit the deferred-categories heading at line 62
    │                                    ("Deferred categories (require /aide:init in Claude Code):")
    │                                    and the closing instruction at line 70 ("Open Claude Code
    │                                    in this project and run /aide:init to finish setup."). New
    │                                    text must reflect the spec's per-category mapping — the
    │                                    heading no longer names a single follow-up command (because
    │                                    the categories now route to different surfaces), and the
    │                                    closing instruction is replaced by per-category guidance
    │                                    rendered next to each entry. Signature unchanged:
    │                                    renderWarning(input: {
    │                                      skipped: readonly InstallResult[],
    │                                      failed: readonly InstallResult[],
    │                                      deferredCategories: readonly string[],
    │                                    }): string
    └── index.test.ts                  ← MODIFY: every literal-string assertion that names
                                         `/aide:init` (lines 31, 37, 60, 83, 109, 114) updates to
                                         the new wording. Suppression rule, border characters,
                                         section ordering, numbering — all preserved.
```

Note on the deferred-categories shape: the spec calls for **per-category
follow-up text**, not a single trailing "Next step" line that names one
command. The implementor must change the renderer's contract for
`deferredCategories` from "an array of category names" to "an array of
self-contained category descriptions that include the follow-up surface
inline." The orchestrator's `deferredCategories()` helper becomes the
single source of truth for the wording.

## Prerequisites

This plan has no hard ordering dependency on the other two plans in the
deprecation epic — every edit here is a text-replacement against existing
strings, and nothing in this scope imports from
`.claude/commands/aide/init.md` or from the `COMMANDS` registry entry the
sibling plans delete. It can run in parallel with `.claude/plan.aide`.

That said, the deprecation only becomes coherent when all three plans have
landed. Stating the soft dependency here for completeness so the
orchestrator's plan-execution log preserves the chain:

- **`src/tools/init/scaffoldCommands/plan.aide` (sibling, MUST run first
  for the epic to be coherent)** — removes the `commands/aide/init` entry
  from the `COMMANDS` registry and the matching `"commands/aide/init"`
  entry from `DOC_PATHS`. Without this, future `aide_init` calls would
  throw on `readCanonicalDoc("commands/aide/init")` and the cli's MCP
  tool would still scaffold an orphan command file. This plan does not
  consume any export from that module — the relationship is product
  coherence, not type/import coupling.

- **`.claude/plan.aide` (sibling, parallel-able with this plan after
  `scaffoldCommands` lands)** — deletes `.claude/commands/aide/init.md`
  and rewrites the three `/aide:init` references in
  `.claude/commands/aide.md`. Independent edits in a different folder
  scope; no contract crosses between this plan and that one.

The cross-module sweep in step 7 of this plan touches files outside
`src/cli/init/` (README + four JSDoc blocks) — those touches are
text-only, do not depend on either sibling plan's code changes, and only
make sense as part of the same epic. See Decisions for the
single-module-scoping deviation rationale.

## Plan

### 1. Update `deferredCategories()` in `src/cli/init/index.ts` to embed per-category follow-up surfaces

Read:
- `coding-playbook/foundations/conventions` — naming, prose clarity, no commented-out code

The orchestrator currently returns category labels in
`deferredCategories(vaultPath)` (lines 34–43) that read like topic names
("Vault path — obsidian MCP entry shell is written but the vault path
is empty; /aide:init will prompt for it"). The spec's per-category
mapping requires each entry to **name the follow-up surface inline**, so
the renderer can drop its single-command "Next step" line and surface
per-category guidance next to the category itself.

The new wording per category, traced to the spec frontmatter (desired
outcomes #1, #5, #8 and undesired #17):

- **Vault path category (only present when `vaultPath` is absent):**
  the follow-up is opening Claude Code and running `/aide` (the
  orchestrator's inline-recovery flow detects the missing path via
  `aide_info` and prompts for it). The category description must name
  `/aide` literally, must not name `/aide:init`, and must include the
  word "orchestrator" so the user understands they're being routed to
  an in-Claude conversation, not a separate slash command.

- **Brain vault scaffolding category (only present when `vaultPath` is
  absent):** the follow-up is the same `/aide` flow — the inline-recovery
  flow seeds the vault directories and playbook hub after the path
  resolves. Prefer wording that makes this clear (e.g. "follows the
  vault path above — same `/aide` run").

- **IDE configuration category (always present unless future `--ide`
  support lands):** the follow-up is re-running the cli with `--ide
  <choice>`. The category description must name `npx aidemd-mcp init
  --ide <choice>` literally so a reader copying the line into their
  shell gets a runnable command.

- [x] 1a. Rewrite the two-branch return inside `deferredCategories()` so
  every string is self-contained guidance. The branch shape is unchanged
  (one entry when `vaultPath` is supplied, three entries when it is
  absent), but the strings themselves shift from labels to instructions.

- [x] 1b. Update the function-level JSDoc on `deferredCategories()` (the
  block at lines 20–33) to remove the literal `/aide:init` references at
  lines 24 and 27. Replace with the spec's per-category routing
  vocabulary: vault-path defers to `/aide` (orchestrator inline
  recovery), IDE defers to re-running the cli with `--ide`. Keep the
  "Single source of truth" sentence — the helper still IS the single
  source of truth for the deferred-categories wording, that part is
  unchanged.

- [x] 1c. Update the runtime "all-clean" completion line at line 225
  from `"Already set up. Run /aide:init in Claude Code to continue."`
  to a string that does not name a removed command. Two acceptable
  shapes the implementor may pick from based on prose flow:
  - `"Already set up."` (terse — no follow-up named because nothing is
    pending; this is the suppression branch's whole point)
  - `"Already set up. Open Claude Code and run /aide to start the
    pipeline."` (longer — names the orchestrator entry but only as a
    "what's next once you're ready to work" pointer, not as a setup
    step). Pick the terse form unless the spec body's "Already set up"
    examples (lines 269–279, 276) were rewritten by the spec writer to
    expect the longer form. Verify by re-reading the spec's good
    examples in step 6 and choosing the form that matches.

- [x] 1d. Update the `--help` text in the IIFE at lines 254–270. The
  `/aide:init` references at lines 264, 267, and 270 all rewrite to the
  per-category surfaces from 1a. Specifically:
  - Line 264 ("/aide:init will prompt for it in Claude Code") → name
    the orchestrator's inline-recovery surface (`/aide`).
  - Line 267 ("deferred to /aide:init") → name the per-category surfaces
    (vault path / brain → `/aide`; IDE → re-run cli with `--ide`).
  - Line 270 ("run /aide:init to finish setup") → describe what the
    warning block now does (lists each pending category with its own
    follow-up surface).

- [x] 1e. Verify post-edit that `src/cli/init/index.ts` contains zero
  occurrences of the literal string `/aide:init`. Run a search across
  the file. Seven occurrences must drop to zero.

### 2. Rewrite the runtime message and JSDoc in `src/cli/init/writeMcpEntry/index.ts`

Read:
- `coding-playbook/foundations/conventions` — JSDoc rules, no commented-out content

The mcp-entry writer's runtime message at line 105 ships verbatim as the
first line of the per-file log (it becomes `mcpResult.message`, which the
orchestrator prints as `[created] .mcp.json — <message>`). That's
user-facing CLI output and falls under the spec's undesired #17
("CLI-emitted text directs the user to a removed slash command"). The
JSDoc at lines 19 and 25 is internal documentation but still drift if
left referencing a deleted surface — and the spec body sweep in step 6
needs the JSDoc to match the new vocabulary so future architects don't
re-derive an outdated mental model.

- [x] 2a. Rewrite the runtime message append at line 105. Current:
  `" — obsidian vault path is a placeholder; run /aide:init to set it"`.
  New text must (a) name the orchestrator's inline-recovery surface
  (`/aide`) instead of `/aide:init`, (b) make clear this is a recoverable
  state (the entry shell IS written), and (c) fit on the same line as
  the rest of the `[created] .mcp.json — ...` log row without wrapping
  awkwardly. Suggested phrasing (verbatim acceptable):
  `" — obsidian vault path is a placeholder; open Claude Code and run /aide to set it"`.
  The implementor may tighten or rephrase as long as the three
  constraints above hold and `/aide:init` does not appear.

- [x] 2b. Rewrite the JSDoc at lines 19 and 25 to match the new
  vocabulary. Line 19's current sentence about the orchestrator
  treating empty path as a hard gate "directing the user to `/aide:init`
  to fill in the real vault path" rewrites to name `/aide` (the
  orchestrator's inline-recovery flow). Line 25's parenthetical "the
  user fixes it via `/aide:init`" rewrites the same way. Keep the
  surrounding JSDoc structure (the explanation of the dual-key check,
  the never-overwrite invariant, the abort-on-malformed-JSON contract)
  intact — only the surface names change.

- [x] 2c. Verify `src/cli/init/writeMcpEntry/index.ts` contains zero
  occurrences of the literal `/aide:init`. Three occurrences must drop
  to zero.

- [x] 2d. Read `src/cli/init/writeMcpEntry/index.test.ts`. If any
  assertion checks for the substring `/aide:init` in the message
  field, update it to the new wording chosen in 2a. If no assertion
  checks for that substring (the test may only assert `.status` and
  rough message shape), leave the test file untouched. Do NOT edit
