### 7. Cross-module reference sweep — README + four JSDoc blocks

Read:
- `coding-playbook/foundations/conventions` — JSDoc rules, prose hygiene
- (no other notes; this step is mechanical text replacement)

This step intentionally crosses out of `src/cli/init/`. Five files
outside this module's scope still carry `/aide:init` references and
none are owned by the other two plans in the deprecation epic. The user's
brief explicitly bundled them into this plan via Option A. See Decisions
for the single-module-scoping deviation rationale.

The five files and their reference counts:

- `README.md` (2 occurrences) — Quick Start section. Line 37 lists what
  `npx` installs; line 42 tells the reader to run `/aide:init` to
  complete setup.
- `src/types/index.ts` (2 occurrences) — JSDoc on `BrainState` (line
  352) and `InfoResult.brain` (line 376). Both name `/aide:init` as the
  remediation when `brain.status` is not `"ok"`.
- `src/tools/info/index.ts` (1 occurrence) — JSDoc on the `info` default
  export (line 42). Same remediation reference as the type docs.
- `src/tools/info/buildBrainState/index.ts` (2 occurrences) — JSDoc on
  the helper (lines 21 and 34). Both name `/aide:init` as the
  remediation when the obsidian entry is missing or malformed.
- `src/tools/init/provisionBrain/index.ts` (1 occurrence) — JSDoc on
  `obsidianMcpEntry` (line 44). Names `/aide:init` as the surface the
  orchestrator routes the user to when the brain path is empty.

Replacement rule per file (the per-category mapping from the spec
applies — JSDoc wording follows the same rule as runtime text):

- README.md line 37 (`Writes the /aide:init slash command...`): the cli
  no longer installs `/aide:init`. Remove the bullet entirely; the cli
  now installs the full canonical-artifact set, which the surrounding
  paragraphs (line 36 covers MCP, line 38 covers the launcher) already
  imply. If the paragraph reads thin without the bullet, replace with
  one that names what the cli DOES install (e.g., "Writes every pipeline
  slash command to `.claude/commands/aide/`").
- README.md line 42 (`After running, open Claude Code and run /aide:init
  to complete setup.`): rewrite to name the orchestrator entry. Suggested
  phrasing: `After running, open Claude Code and run /aide — the
  orchestrator will prompt for any setup the cli could not finish (vault
  path, IDE choice).`
- All four JSDoc files (types/info/buildBrainState/provisionBrain): the
  remediation is the orchestrator's inline-recovery flow (`/aide`), not
  `/aide:init`. Replace each "run `/aide:init`" or "/aide:init"
  occurrence with text that names `/aide` as the remediation surface and
  briefly clarifies that the orchestrator's inline-recovery flow detects
  the missing/broken state and prompts the user. Preserve the
  surrounding sentence structure and tone — only the surface name and
  one or two surrounding clauses change.

- [x] 7a. Apply the README rewrites at lines 37 and 42 per the rule
  above. After the edit, re-read the Quick Start section end-to-end and
  confirm the prose flows. Quick Start is a high-stakes onboarding
  surface; if a sentence feels stilted after the edit, rephrase the
  surrounding context too — minimum diff is not the priority for the
  README, prose quality is.

- [x] 7b. Apply the JSDoc rewrites in `src/types/index.ts` (lines 352
  and 376), `src/tools/info/index.ts` (line 42),
  `src/tools/info/buildBrainState/index.ts` (lines 21 and 34), and
  `src/tools/init/provisionBrain/index.ts` (line 44) per the rule
  above. Preserve the JSDoc block structure; do not reorder or rename
  parameters or types; do not edit anything other than the
  remediation-surface naming.

- [x] 7c. Final cross-repo verification: search the entire repo for the
  literal string `/aide:init` and confirm the ONLY remaining
  occurrences are in:
  - `src/cli/init/.aide` line 108 (the undesired-outcome bullet that
    names `/aide:init` as the example of a removed surface to forbid —
    this is intentional and stays);
  - the three sibling plan.aide files
    (`src/cli/init/plan.aide` itself, `.claude/plan.aide`,
    `src/tools/init/scaffoldCommands/plan.aide`,
    `src/cli/init/todo.aide`, `src/tools/init/todo.aide`,
    `src/tools/info/todo.aide`) — these are planning artifacts and
    deletion logs that intentionally name the deprecated surface; they
    are out of scope for this sweep.
  - `.aide/review.md` if present — explicitly out of scope per the
    user's brief.
  - any historical references inside `src/tools/info/.aide` or
    `src/tools/init/.aide` (the sister specs) — those are owned by
    their own future spec rewrites, not by this plan.

  Exclude `.git/`, `node_modules/`, `dist/`, and the generated
  `package-lock.json` from the search. Any production-code, test, or
  README occurrence outside the allowlist above is a missed reference
  and the implementor must close it before declaring step 7 done.

### 8. Verification — full test suite, type check, and final grep

Read:
- `coding-playbook/workflow/testing` — `tsc --noEmit` workflow, vitest scripts
- `coding-playbook/foundations/conventions` — code hygiene

This step is the gate before declaring the plan done. All three checks
must pass; any failure escalates back to `/aide:plan` rather than being
patched in-flight.

- [ ] 8a. Run `npm test` (project's vitest invocation). Every test in
  `src/cli/init/`, `src/tools/`, and `src/lib/` must pass. Pay
  particular attention to:
  - `src/cli/init/index.test.ts` — `DEFERRED_CATEGORIES` constant is
    the most likely failure source; if it diverges from the
    orchestrator by even one character every equality assertion that
    uses it will fail.
  - `src/cli/init/renderWarning/index.test.ts` — the line-by-line
    `.join("\n")` expected blocks are sensitive to leading whitespace,
    blank lines, and border placement.
  - `src/cli/init/writeMcpEntry/index.test.ts` — only relevant if step
    2d updated an assertion.

- [x] 8b. Run `tsc --noEmit` (or the project's equivalent type-check
  script). No new type errors should appear; this plan changes string
  values and JSDoc text only, so the type surface is unchanged.

- [x] 8c. Run a final cross-repo grep for the literal `/aide:init` and
  re-validate the allowlist from step 7c. The acceptance criterion is
  zero matches outside the allowlist. If the implementor finds an
  occurrence the plan didn't anticipate, STOP and escalate to
  `/aide:plan` — the sweep was incomplete and the spec writer needs to
  decide whether the missed reference should be removed (the typical
  case) or preserved (rare, only for historical artifacts).

- [x] 8d. Confirm by re-reading `src/cli/init/.aide`'s undesired #17
  bullet (line 108) that the runtime cli output (warning block,
  per-file log, --help, completion line) no longer names `/aide:init`.
  This is the spec's explicit testable assertion, not just a cleanup
  goal.

## Decisions

- **The renderer's contract for `deferredCategories` shifts from "labels"
  to "self-contained instructions."** The spec's per-category mapping
  forces this — vault path routes to `/aide`, IDE routes to a cli re-run
  with `--ide`, drift routes to `/aide:upgrade`. There is no longer one
  command that handles all three. The cleanest place to encode the
  per-category surfaces is the `deferredCategories()` helper in the
  orchestrator, which already exists as the single source of truth (per
  its own JSDoc). Pushing the wording into that helper means the
  renderer becomes a dumb numbered-list printer for whatever strings it
  receives, which preserves its purity (no I/O, no surface-specific
  knowledge) and lets the orchestrator carry the spec-specific
  vocabulary. This is why step 1 owns the heavy wording lift and step 3
  is mostly subtraction.

- **The renderer's "Next step" closing line is deleted, not rewritten.**
  Once each category carries its own follow-up inline, a single trailing
  line that names ONE command would either contradict the category-level
  guidance (which surface should it name?) or repeat what's already
  visible (which is noise). Deletion is the correct answer; the closing
  border bookends the block visually without needing prose.

- **The cross-module sweep in step 7 deviates from `plan-aide.md`'s
  single-module-scoping rule.** The rule states that a plan governs only
  the module it lives in, and a feature spanning multiple modules
  produces multiple plans. The five files in step 7 sit outside
  `src/cli/init/` and would, by strict interpretation, require five
  separate plans. The user explicitly bundled them into this plan via
  Option A in their brief, with three justifications all met here:
  (a) the cleanup is mechanical text-replacement with no behavioural or
  type changes; (b) the alternative is four-plus micro-plans for one-line
  edits each, which is process overhead with negative pedagogical value
  (each plan would be 90% boilerplate); (c) every file in step 7 is
  legitimately downstream of the deprecation — these references only
  exist because `/aide:init` existed, and removing them is part of the
  same coherent change. The deviation is recorded here so a future
  architect reading this plan understands why the rule was bent and is
  not licensed to bend it elsewhere.

- **`writeMcpEntry/index.ts` is in the primary scope, not the
  cross-module sweep.** Even though the user's brief listed it as a
  primary-scope concern via reference to lines 62 and 70 of
  `renderWarning/index.ts`, the writer also has three `/aide:init`
  references (JSDoc lines 19, 25 and a runtime message at line 105). All
  three are inside `src/cli/init/` (the writer is a sibling of the
  orchestrator, not a cross-module concern). Step 2 owns them
  explicitly so the implementor doesn't miss them by reading the brief
  literally and stopping at the renderer.

- **The architecture of the cli is unchanged.** No helper extraction, no
  folder restructuring, no orchestrator/helper boundary changes. The
  user's brief explicitly defers the services-layer refactor that would
  move helpers out of `src/tools/init/` into a shared services package;
  this plan honors that deferral. Future readers seeing the bent
  single-module-scoping rule must not interpret it as license for
  broader restructuring — every step here is a string change or a
  closing-line deletion.

- **No new tests.** The spec's behavioral contract (warning block layout,
  suppression rule, idempotency, MCP merge invariants) is unchanged;
  only the strings it emits change. Adding new test cases would be
  test-pollution against an unchanged contract. Existing tests that
  assert string content get updated assertions (steps 4 and 5); existing
  tests that assert structure stay untouched.

- **The completion-line wording in step 1c is the only judgment call
  the implementor must make in-flight.** Two acceptable forms are
  enumerated; the choice is constrained by what the spec body's good
  examples (lines 269–279) actually show after the spec writer's
  rewrite. If the spec body unambiguously expects one form, pick that
  one; if it's still ambiguous after step 6's body rewrite drafts the
  examples, pick the terse `"Already set up."` form because it most
  cleanly satisfies the suppression-rule contract (the whole point of
  suppressing the warning is "nothing pending — nothing to say").

- **Read lists in this plan are short by design.** Most steps are
  text replacement against existing strings; the playbook conventions
  that govern this work are concentrated in two notes
  (`coding-playbook/foundations/conventions` and
  `coding-playbook/workflow/testing`). Reading more would burn context
  on conventions that don't apply to this kind of mechanical sweep.

## Risks

- **The implementor picks a completion-line wording in step 1c that
  diverges from the spec body's example wording in step 6c.** Both steps
  pick wording independently; if 1c picks the terse form and 6c writes
  the longer form into the example, the example claims the cli prints
  something the cli does not actually print. Mitigation: do step 6
  AFTER step 1, and copy the chosen line verbatim from the orchestrator
  source into the example. The plan's step ordering enforces this, but
  a careless implementor could draft step 6's examples against memory of
  what step 1 said rather than re-reading the source.

- **The cross-module sweep in step 7 misses a reference.** The
  acceptance grep in step 7c is the gate; the plan lists explicit
  allowlist files for the grep to ignore. If a NEW reference has crept
  into the codebase between this plan being written and being executed
  (a recent commit added a JSDoc that names `/aide:init`), the grep
  catches it and step 8c forces an escalation rather than a silent miss.
  This is the intended behavior — better an escalation than a missed
  reference.

- **The renderer's structural change (deleting the "Next step" line)
  is more than a text edit.** It alters the exact line count and
  ordering of the rendered block. Any test that asserts the full block
  via `.join("\n")` will break unless updated, and step 4 lists every
  such assertion explicitly. If a NEW test has been added since this
  plan was written that asserts block content, the implementor must
  update it too — `npm test` in step 8a will surface the failure, but
  the failure mode might be misread as "my edit broke something" rather
  than "the test predates this plan and needs the same surgery as the
  others." Mitigation: step 8a's instructions name the renderer test as
  the most likely failure source; the implementor sees the failure in
  context.

- **Future host projects already have `init.md` on disk and the
  warning block won't tell them.** This plan does not own the cleanup
  of orphan `init.md` files in host projects — that is the job of
  `/aide:upgrade`'s deletion-handling logic, which is out of scope here.
  The cli's warning block, after this plan, will not flag a host
  project's existing `init.md` as drift because the cli's
  canonical-artifact set no longer includes it. A user on an existing
  install gets no signal from the cli that they have a stale file. The
  `.claude/plan.aide` Risks section already flags this; it is recorded
  here too because the user-facing surface most affected (the warning
  block) lives in the scope of this plan.

- **The intentional `/aide:init` reference in undesired #17 (line 108
  of the spec) is a load-bearing exception to the sweep.** That bullet
  exists precisely to forbid CLI output that names removed slash
  commands and uses `/aide:init` as the canonical example. Step 7c's
  allowlist preserves it. If a future architect reads this plan and
  decides to "complete the sweep" by removing that reference too, the
  spec's testability contract for undesired #17 weakens — the bullet
  becomes self-referential ("don't name removed commands; for example,
  don't name <empty>"). Leave it.
